<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1933</h1><p class="ref-lede">1933 in world history: 47 events, 7 births, 21 deaths, 1 work, 4 states founded and 2 that came to an end. 719 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a><a href="/years/1930">1930</a><a href="/years/1931">1931</a><a href="/years/1932">1932</a><a href="/years/1933" aria-current="page">1933</a><a href="/years/1934">1934</a><a href="/years/1935">1935</a><a href="/years/1936">1936</a><a href="/years/1937">1937</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Construction of the Golden Gate Bridge begins</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Work began on a bridge across the Golden Gate strait at the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> Bay. Opened in 1937, its 1,280-metre main span was the longest in the world until 1964.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Hitler appointed Chancellor of Germany</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President Hindenburg made the Nazi leader Chancellor, with conservatives expecting to control him. Within months Hitler had suspended civil liberties and established a one-party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First issue of News-Week is published</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The weekly news magazine, later Newsweek, was launched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> as a rival to Time. It became one of the leading American news weeklies of the 20th century.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">USS Ranger, the first US ship designed as a carrier, is launched</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Ranger, the first US vessel designed and built from the keel up as an aircraft carrier, was launched at Newport News, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Commissioned in 1934, she spent most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> in the Atlantic and provided air support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-27">27 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Fire destroys the Reichstag in Berlin</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The German parliament building burned and a young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> communist, Marinus van der Lubbe, was arrested. Next day Hitler secured a decree suspending civil liberties, a key step to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-4">4 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Franklin D. Roosevelt inaugurated in the depths of the Depression</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With banks failing nationwide, Roosevelt told Americans 'the only thing we have to fear is fear itself'. In his first hundred days Congress passed a wave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> legislation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-20">20 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Himmler announces the Dachau concentration camp</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>'s police chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> announced a camp for political prisoners near Dachau, opened two days later. It became the model for the Nazi camp system; over 40,000 died there.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-23">23 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Reichstag passes the Enabling Act</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">With communists barred and others intimidated, the German parliament allowed Hitler's cabinet to make laws without it. Only the Social Democrats voted against, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> had a legal basis.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nazi regime stages a nationwide boycott of Jewish businesses</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">SA men stood outside Jewish-owned shops, offices and surgeries across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to turn customers away. The one-day boycott was the first nationally coordinated anti-Jewish action of the new regime.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">British biplanes make the first flight over Everest</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Two Westland aircraft of the Houston expedition flew over the summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-everest">Mount Everest</a>, crews breathing bottled oxygen in open cockpits. They brought back the first aerial photographs of the peak.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/theatre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theatre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First drive-in cinema opens in New Jersey</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Richard Hollingshead opened his 'Automobile Movie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>' at Pennsauken, near Camden. Drive-ins became a fixture of post-war American suburban life.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-14">14 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nazi Germany becomes a one-party state</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Law against the Formation of New Parties declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> the only legal party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Less than six months after Hitler became chancellor, organised political opposition had been outlawed.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-22">22 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Wiley Post completes the first solo flight around the world</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Wiley Post landed at Floyd Bennett Field, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, after circling the globe alone in 7 days, 18 hours and 49 minutes. An autopilot and radio direction-finder made the feat possible.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-5">5 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Prohibition ends in the United States</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> became the 36th state to ratify the 21st Amendment, repealing national Prohibition. The ban on alcohol had lasted almost 14 years and fed a boom in organised crime.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Reichstag Fire Decree was an emergency decree issued by President Paul von Hindenburg on 28 February 1933, the day after the Reichstag building was set on fire.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933">Enabling Act of 1933</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Enabling Act of 1933 was a law passed by the German Reichstag on 23 March 1933 that allowed Adolf Hitler's government to issue laws without parliament's approval, even ones departing from the constitution.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/simele-massacre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simele-massacre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/simele-massacre">Simele massacre</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Simele massacre was the killing of Assyrian villagers by the Iraqi army in northern Iraq in August 1933, centred on the town of Simele near Dohuk.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/tanggu-truce" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanggu-truce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/tanggu-truce">Tanggu Truce</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Tanggu Truce was the ceasefire signed at Tanggu, near Tianjin, on 31 May 1933 between China and Japan after Japanese forces had pushed south of the Great Wall.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/roca-runciman-treaty" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roca-runciman-treaty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/roca-runciman-treaty">Roca–Runciman Treaty</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Roca–Runciman Treaty was a trade agreement signed in 1933 by Argentine Vice-President Julio Argentino Roca Jr and the British trade minister Walter Runciman.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/bunyoro-agreement-1933">Bunyoro Agreement (1933)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Bunyoro Agreement was signed in 1933 between the British colonial government of the Uganda Protectorate and Omukama Tito Winyi IV.</p></div></li></ol></section>
